A||||5||BAM-BAM||New Question!!! Steering Wheel Alignment???||||5||
Z||000000||BAM-BAM||12-24-2001||12:14 AM|||| Ok, I changed the stock GT wheel out to a Grant Signature wheel. I lined everything up and the wheel is now askew to the right, just barely. I tried turning the hub back to the left one notch on the column and now it's way out to the left. How do you align the steering wheel to the front wheels? Is there a process by which this can be done???  Any help appreciated.<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>85GT 3.4L <BR>Eibachs and Poly's<BR>17" Konig Monsoons<BR>Kumho Ecstas<BR>Interior is all "Mike'd" up||||reg||5
Z||000001||Devotshka||12-24-2001||12:21 AM||||Check here <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/016633.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/016633.html</A> <P>Your best bet all points considered is to have it realigned, however there is instruction there for a quick fix too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Hows that 3.4 coming along BAM-BAM?  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||12-24-2001||12:49 AM||||I had the same problem when I installed my Grant wheel. I had to remove the lock plate- that little disk with the holes around the edge that locks the steering wheel when the key is off- and grind out the master spline on it. Then I could move it and the horn wire to where ever it needed to be for the steering wheel to be aligned. It still took some trial and error to get it right, but it can be done. You can also use a "rat tail" file to make the hole where the horn lead comes through the lock plate a little longer and possible get the wheel back centered that way.||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||12-24-2001||02:14 AM||||I decided that you'd figure it out in the long run.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||12-24-2001||07:06 AM||||the rite way is a little tougher and requires you to get under the front. You can change the position of the wheel by adjusting tie rod ends. turn one side out and the other side in the exact same number of turns. The steering shaft is marked as to where it is supposed to be, shouldnt be grinding or modifying it. That will in the least affect your turn sig switch.||||reg||1
Z||000005||XDrewX||12-24-2001||03:33 PM||||Man,  I thought it was just me.....<P>I have installed 3 grant steering wheels into three different models of Pontiac cars, <BR>and each one of them the wheel was crooked after installation !!!<P>Now in each of these cars the stock wheel was perfectly aligned, before I removed it.<P>I think I am going to try and contact Grant, I thought this was just one of those Murphy's Law things.  I am a magnet for those things. LOL<P>Peace<P>DAP<P>||||reg||1
